WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.300
Energy first: sleep, a minimum protocol and how capacity grows.

2
00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:09.620
It is half past one. Galih has an interview on Friday and a thesis chapter due, and for a week he has slept five hours a night.

3
00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:17.980
The trouble is what a tired brain loses first: attention, working memory and control of mood. Exactly the skills an interview tests.

4
00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:26.850
Usable energy comes from four systems. Sleep stores what you studied. Movement lifts mood and focus. Fuel: regular meals, and coffee early. And recovery: a real off-switch. Scrolling does not count.

5
00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:35.380
Galih has made a health plan before. It was perfect, and it lasted two days. A plan built for your best week breaks in your worst one.

6
00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:42.100
So this time he writes a floor: four lines small enough to keep in a bad week. In a crunch week the floor may shrink. But it never goes to zero.

7
00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:50.520
Capacity grows the way a muscle does: a demand that stretches you a little, then enough rest to adapt. The growth happens in the rest, not in the effort.

8
00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:58.760
Overload sends early signals, weeks before real trouble. Sleep that no longer refreshes you. Irritation at small things. And pulling away from people you normally enjoy.

9
00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:07.000
Subtract first: drop one commitment for two weeks and protect your sleep. Then talk, to a friend, a mentor or a counsellor. And if a low mood stays most days for two weeks, seeing a professional is the competent move.

10
00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:13.500
Friday morning. Four nights inside his sleep window, and a walk to campus. The interview is the same. The brain he brings to it is not.

11
00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:22.890
And on a day that feels harder than it should, he checks his levers before he blames his character: sleep, heat, food, mental load.

12
00:01:23.290 --> 00:01:30.290
A floor you never break beats a ceiling you never reach. Capacity grows in the rest after effort.

13
00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:35.090
Now it is your turn. Write your four lines: a sleep window, movement, one real meal, one person.
